course: world
lesson: 84
title: Mirrors, shadows and colours
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A mirror reflects light like a ball bouncing off a wall: the light leaves the mirror at the same angle as it arrived. In a mirror, writing looks back to front, which is why AMBULANCE is printed reversed on the front of ambulances, so drivers read it the right way in their mirrors.
explain: A periscope uses two mirrors to bend the path of light twice, so you can see over a wall or out of a submarine. Curved mirrors change the picture: a mirror that bulges outwards shows a wide view, so it is used in car wing mirrors and shop corners.
explain: Shadows change during the day because the Sun's position in the sky changes. In the morning and evening the Sun is low and shadows are long; around midday it is highest and shadows are shortest. A shadow always points away from the light.
explain: Light bends when it passes from water or glass into air. That is why a straw in a glass of water looks broken. A glass prism bends each colour by a different amount and spreads white light into a rainbow of colours.
explain: A rainbow appears when the Sun is behind you and rain is in front of you: each raindrop bends and reflects the sunlight and splits it into colours. Its colours are always in the same order: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo and violet.
fact: A mirror reflects light at the same angle as the light arrives.
fact: In a mirror, writing appears back to front.
fact: A periscope uses two mirrors to let you see over things.
fact: A mirror that bulges outwards shows a wider view than a flat one.
fact: Shadows are longest in the morning and evening and shortest around midday.
fact: A shadow always points away from the source of light.
fact: A sundial tells the time from the position of a shadow.
fact: Light bends when it passes between water, glass and air.
fact: A prism splits white light into the colours of the rainbow.
fact: A rainbow appears when the Sun is behind you and rain is in front of you.
fact: The colours of the rainbow are red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo and violet, with red on the outside edge of the bow.
fact: A red filter lets only red light through and blocks the other colours.
exercise: A ray of light hits a flat mirror at a slant. How does it leave the mirror? | At the same angle on the other side | Straight back the same way that it came | Straight through the glass of the mirror | Along the flat surface of the mirror
exercise: Why is the word AMBULANCE written back to front on the front of ambulances? | So drivers ahead read it correctly in their mirrors | So people walking behind it can read it | Because it is easier to paint that way round | So it looks the same in daylight and darkness
exercise: What does a periscope use to let you see over a wall? | Two mirrors | Two magnets | One thick lens | A small camera
exercise: Why do shops put a bulging mirror high in a corner? | It shows a wide view of the shop | It makes everything look bigger | It makes the shop brighter at night | It shows things as they were before
exercise: When is your shadow shortest on a sunny day? | Around midday | Early in the morning | Late in the afternoon | Just before sunset
exercise: In the evening the Sun is in the west. Which way does your shadow point? | Towards the east | Towards the west | Straight down under your feet | Towards the Sun
exercise: What old instrument tells the time from a shadow? | A sundial | A compass | A barometer | An hourglass
exercise: Why does a straw in a glass of water look broken? | Light bends as it leaves the water | The water really bends the straw | The glass makes a shadow on the straw | Our eyes cannot see through water
exercise: What does a glass prism do to white light? | Spreads it out into rainbow colours | Turns it all into red light | Makes it brighter and much whiter than before | Stops it completely
exercise: Where must the Sun be for you to see a rainbow? | Behind you, with rain in front of you | In front of you, behind the rain | Straight above your head at midday | Hidden completely behind thick cloud
exercise: Which colour is on the outside edge of a rainbow? | Red | Violet | Green | Yellow
exercise: What does a red filter do to white light? | Lets only the red light through | Turns all the light into white | Adds red to all the other colours | Lets every colour through except red
exercise: Why are long shadows seen early in the morning? | The Sun is low in the sky | The Sun is closer to the Earth | The Sun is weaker in the morning | The air is thicker in the morning
exercise: Why do you see a bright patch when sunlight hits a mirror and bounces onto a wall? | The mirror reflects the light to the wall | The mirror makes its own light | The mirror heats the wall until it glows | The mirror soaks up the light and lets it go
test: You want to shine a torch beam onto a toy hidden around a corner. What can help? | A flat mirror placed at the corner | A sheet of thick cardboard in the corner | A clear glass window pane at the corner | A black cloth hung right across the corner
test: A flagpole's shadow points to the west. Where in the sky is the Sun? | In the east | In the west | Straight overhead | In the north at night
test: Which is true about rainbows? | You see one when you face away from the Sun | You see one when you look straight at the Sun | Rainbows only appear when there is no rain | Every rainbow has its colours in a new order
test: You put a coin in an empty cup and move back until it just hides behind the rim. A friend pours in water and the coin appears. Why? | Light from the coin bends as it leaves the water | The water lifts the coin up off the bottom towards the rim | The water makes the coin shine more brightly | The cup becomes see-through when it is wet
test: Why are mirrors that bulge outwards used on cars? | They show more of the road behind | They make cars behind look closer | They help the car go faster | They make the driver's face look smaller
test: A child stands in the same spot at 9 o'clock, at 12 o'clock and at 3 o'clock on a sunny day. When is the shadow shortest? | At 12 o'clock | At 9 o'clock | At 3 o'clock | It is the same all day
test: You shine white light through a red filter and then through a green filter. What do you see? | Almost no light at all | Bright yellow light like the Sun | Bright white light | Red and green stripes
test: A sailor in a submarine wants to see ships on the surface. Which tool helps? | A periscope | A sundial | A prism | A magnifying glass
test: Which shows that light bends when it changes material? | A spoon in water looks bent | A shadow points away from a lamp | A mirror shows writing reversed | A black shirt gets hot in sunshine
test: why is my shadow massive this arvo but tiny at lunch | Shadows are longest in morning and evening and shortest around midday | The Sun gets bigger in the afternoon | Shadows grow longer as you get tired | The ground gets darker as the day goes on
test: wanna see a rainbow, where's the sun gotta be | Behind you, with rain in front of you | In front of you, with rain behind you | Right above you at midday | Hidden behind thick clouds
test: Mia says a mirror flips things upside down. true? | No, it shows writing back to front, not upside down | Yes, everything in a mirror is upside down | Yes, but only in a big mirror | No, a mirror shows things exactly as they are
test: my mate reckons a red filter turns all light red. right? | No, it lets only red light through and blocks the other colours | Yes, it paints every colour red | Yes, but only white light turns red | No, it lets every colour through except red
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reflection of light by a mirror? | Light is reflected at the same angle at which it arrives | Light is reflected straight back whatever its angle | Light is absorbed and none is reflected | Light is split into the colours of the rainbow
exam: Identify the instrument which employs two mirrors to permit vision over obstacles. | A periscope | A prism | A sundial | A filter
exam: It is given that the Sun stands behind an observer and rain falls before him. Ascertain the phenomenon which may thereby be observed. | A rainbow | An eclipse | A mirage | A shooting star
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a red filter. | It permits only red light to pass and blocks the other colours | It permits all colours save red to pass | It converts all colours into red light | It blocks red light and permits white light to pass
